\details{
The local bounding box is centered at zero before object transforms,
spanning \code{-c(width, height, depth) / 2} to \code{c(width, height, depth) / 2}.
The density fades to vacuum at all six faces; the box has no visible surface.
An unrotated, unscaled cloud with base altitude \code{b} has \code{y = b + height / 2}.
The center describes the box, not the irregular density's center of mass.

Positions and dimensions use scene units. Setting the dimensions rebuilds
the field and normalizes extinction by \code{height}. Applying \code{scale} stretches
the existing volume without renormalizing extinction, so stretching it along
a ray increases that ray's optical depth. Standard \code{\link[=group_objects]{group_objects()}},
\code{\link[=animate_objects]{animate_objects()}}, and \code{\link[=create_instances]{create_instances()}} operations transform the cloud
using the same object machinery as other closed shapes.

Animation translates the sampling coordinates through fixed three-dimensional
Perlin fields. Broad and fine features share a direction selected by
\code{animation_seed}; one time unit travels 0.1 times the longest box dimension.
Broad domes, the base profile, and edge fades stay fixed while noise passes
through them. This is procedural animation, not a fluid simulation; cloud
mass is not conserved. Use \code{x}, \code{y}, and \code{z} to move the whole cloud.

Rebuild the cloud with a new \code{t} value for each rendered frame.
\code{\link[=animate_objects]{animate_objects()}} animates its transform; it does not evolve the density
within a frame or during the shutter interval.

Cloud scattering is separate from \code{\link[=sky_light]{sky_light()}}'s clear-air atmospheric haze.
Avoid intersecting, non-nested cloud boxes, including their empty edge cells:
these are separate medium boundaries. Use one larger field for a connected
bank. The attached \code{\link[=grid_medium]{grid_medium()}} is stored in
\code{object$shape_info[[1]]$medium} for further density or scattering adjustments.
